SEC filings.content:
{
  "score": 38,
  "score_rationale": "CoreWeave's SEC filings reveal a deeply leveraged balance sheet ($17.8B total debt, D/E 3.75x), a current ratio of 0.31x against $8.06B short-term debt, and $7.7B Q1 CapEx against negative FCF of -$4.71B — with covenant disclosures, RPO composition, and debt maturity schedules remaining materially opaque in available data, warranting a below-average filing quality/transparency score.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "finding": "Current ratio collapsed to 0.31x in Q1 2026 with $8.06B in short-term debt against only $3.02B cash, implying a structural liquidity shortfall of ~$5B absent refinancing or new capital raises.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /balance-sheet-statement] Q1 2026: Cash $3.02B, ST Debt $8.06B, Current Ratio 0.31"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Gross margin has compressed approximately 10 percentage points from 75.6% in Q4 2024 to 65.5% in Q1 2026, a trend that is accelerating and whose floor management has not publicly quantified.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /income-statement] Q4 2024 GM 75.6% vs Q1 2026 GM 65.5%; sequential compression in Q3 and Q4 2025 also visible"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Free cash flow turned sharply more negative in Q1 2026 at -$4.71B driven by $7.7B CapEx, the single largest quarterly capital spend recorded, indicating the GPU acquisition cycle is still accelerating even as debt servicing costs consume operating cash flow.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement] Q1 2026: CapEx -$7.70B, FCF -$4.71B, OCF +$2.98B"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Interest expense reached $0.54B in the most recent quarter, annualizing to ~$2.2B, which exceeds EBIT on any trailing quarter basis and will continue to suppress net income regardless of revenue growth trajectory.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /income-statement] Latest interest expense $0.54B; Q1 2026 operating margin -6.9% on $2.08B revenue implies operating loss ~$143M"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Insider activity shows zero purchases against $13.2M in net sells, entirely concentrated in Chief Strategy Officer Brian Venturo executing six tranches on May 15, 2026 at prices $107–$111, providing no affirmative conviction signal from insiders proximate to the IPO lock-up expiry.",
      "evidence": "[Source: FMP /insider-trading] Aggregate buys $0.00M vs sells $13.20M; all 6 transactions from Venturo on 2026-05-15"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": "CoreWeave's SEC filings present a company executing a high-velocity infrastructure buildout financed almost entirely through debt, with disclosure quality that obscures several of the most material risks an institutional investor needs to evaluate.\n\n**Debt Structure and Covenant Risk**: The $8.06B classified as short-term debt is the single most alarming line item in the filings. At a current ratio of 0.31x, the company cannot service near-term maturities from existing cash. The critical question — whether these GPU-collateralized facilities contain financial maintenance covenants tied to utilization, LTV ratios, or revenue coverage — remains unanswered from publicly available disclosure. GPU assets collateralizing these facilities are subject to rapid technological obsolescence (H100 → H200 → Blackwell cycles compress useful life and liquidation value), creating a potential LTV squeeze that could trigger covenant violations even if operating performance holds. Management has not provided granular maturity waterfall disclosure in accessible filing summaries, which is itself a red flag for a company with $17.8B total debt [Source: FMP /balance-sheet-statement].\n\n**RPO and Customer Concentration**: The previously flagged question about RPO composition and Microsoft termination provisions remains unresolved. CoreWeave's revenue is heavily concentrated — Microsoft has been identified in prior disclosures as representing a substantial portion of backlog — and if the underlying contracts contain termination-for-convenience or volume-reduction clauses, the $55.9B 2030 revenue consensus estimate becomes speculative. The company's ability to refinance the short-term debt wall depends substantially on demonstrating contracted future cash flows, making RPO quality thesis-load-bearing [Source: FMP /analyst-estimates].\n\n**Gross Margin Deterioration**: The 10pp GM compression from 75.6% (Q4 2024) to 65.5% (Q1 2026) is not adequately explained in available disclosure summaries. Three plausible mechanisms — accelerated depreciation on newer GPU vintages shortening useful life assumptions, mix shift toward lower-margin CPU/storage products, or pricing pressure from hyperscaler competition (AWS, Azure, GCP) — each carry different implications for the margin floor. If depreciation is the driver, margins may stabilize as the fleet matures; if pricing pressure, the trajectory could continue to 55-60%. Management has not quantified a floor, which is a material disclosure gap [Source: FMP /income-statement quarterly series].\n\n**Capital Structure Trajectory**: Total debt grew from approximately $11.05B (Q2 2025) to $17.84B (Q1 2026) in three quarters, an increase of $6.8B. CapEx in the same period totaled roughly $14.1B. The implied asset base expansion suggests CoreWeave is financing a significant portion of GPU purchases off-balance-sheet or through operating leases that may not be fully visible. D/E ratio declining from 4.85x to 3.75x sequentially appears to reflect equity issuance (IPO proceeds) rather than debt reduction [Source: FMP /balance-sheet-statement].\n\n**Filing Quality Assessment**: CoreWeave's filings, as a recently public company (IPO 2025), show limited disclosure maturity. The absence of a detailed debt maturity schedule, covenant summary, and RPO disaggregation in investor-accessible summaries reflects either early-stage IR infrastructure or deliberate opacity. The FMP rating of D+ (1/5) is consistent with this assessment. Analysts appear to be underweighting these structural risks — 16 Buys vs 10 Holds with an average PT of $127 — likely anchoring to the revenue growth trajectory without adequately pricing refinancing and covenant risk.",
  "data_gaps": [
    "Complete debt maturity waterfall for $17.84B total debt including covenant terms, collateral specifics, and any cross-default provisions across GPU-secured facilities — not available in FMP summary data and requires full 10-K/10-Q review",
    "RPO disaggregation by customer, contract tenor, and termination provisions — critical for assessing whether the $55.9B 2030 revenue consensus is contractually supported or at-risk",
    "Depreciation/amortization schedule by GPU vintage and fleet cohort, required to isolate whether GM compression is structural (pricing) or mechanical (depreciation acceleration on newer hardware)"
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "question_text": "The $8.06B short-term debt classification implies maturities within 12 months of Q1 2026 filing — does the 10-Q/10-K debt footnote confirm these are GPU equipment financing facilities maturing in 2026-2027, and do any contain LTV maintenance covenants requiring GPU collateral to maintain a minimum percentage of outstanding principal?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    },
    {
      "question_text": "What is the total RPO balance as of Q1 2026, and what percentage is attributable to Microsoft — and critically, do the Microsoft contracts contain volume flexibility provisions that would allow drawdown of committed capacity below contracted minimums without triggering material breach?",
      "priority": 1,
      "auto_answerable": false
    },
    {
      "question_text": "Is the gross margin compression from 75.6% to 65.5% primarily attributable to changes in depreciation policy or useful life assumptions for GPU assets (i.e., a mechanical accounting effect from fleet vintage mix), versus competitive pricing pressure — and has management provided any guidance on a margin floor in the S-1 or subsequent earnings disclosures?",
      "priority": 2,
      "auto_answerable": false
    }
  ],
  "resolved_questions": [
    {
      "question_id": "ca7dd0b8-6c7c-4691-96f4-e38644b6c2ef",
      "answer_text": "Partially resolvable from available data: The quarterly income statement series confirms GM compressed from 75.6% (Q4 2024) to 65.5% (Q1 2026), with the steepest drops in Q3 2025 and Q1 2026. The compression coincides with the largest CapEx quarters ($2.39B, $4.06B, $7.70B), suggesting accelerated depreciation on newer GPU vintages is a likely contributor — but pricing pressure cannot be ruled out. Operating margins went from +15.1% (Q4 2024) to -6.9% (Q1 2026), with the deterioration accelerating even as revenue grew 111% YoY, pointing to cost structure scaling faster than revenue. Management has not publicly disclosed a margin floor in available data; this question remains materially unresolved and requires full filing footnote review."
    }
  ]
}

