ionately.\n\nThe unresolved lock-up expiration schedule remains a critical unknown. CRWV went public in late March/early April 2026, which places the typical 180-day lock-up expiration in September–October 2026. If pre-IPO holders—including Blackstone, Magnetar, and Nvidia-affiliated entities—gain the right to sell in that window, the float expansion could be structurally destabilizing. The current float is presumably a small fraction of total shares outstanding given the IPO structure, meaning any lock-up-driven supply surge would hit a relatively thin secondary market.\n\nAnalyst consensus remains constructive (Buy, avg PT $127.47, +22.8% from current), but this is lagging technical reality. No analyst downgraded or cut their price target in the eight most recent actions—all were maintenance calls. This is consistent with analysts being anchored to fundamental models rather than responding to technical deterioration. The FMP Rating of D+ (1/5 overall) is a blunt but directionally accurate signal on the composite quality of the name.\n\nIn summary: CRWV is in technical breakdown mode approaching a critical support cluster, with institutional distribution signals, zero insider buying, a dangerously high beta, and an unresolved lock-up overhang. The risk/reward for a technical entry here is poor until the stock either stabilizes above $100 with declining volume or produces an oversold RSI bounce from sub-30 levels.",
